{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law causes based on criminal convictions and civil liability (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(o)-(r))","requirement":"A landlord may evict when a tenant is convicted of, or civilly found liable for, assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, theft on the premises, drug activity or human trafficking. A three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit is barred after two years.","key_value":"3 days' notice to quit; 2-year time limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the Eviction Law, including a tenant who knowingly permits such a convicted person to reside on the premises continuously or occasionally.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Tenancies covered by the New Jersey Eviction Law"},"exemptions":"A tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for use or possession of drugs to reside at the property (civil-liability ground).","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes and notice for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"In an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling the landlord must prove one of six listed causes; three months' notice to quit is required for at-will and year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month, and three days for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating causes.","key_value":"3 months' / 1 month / 3 days' notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal only when a court issues an order for eviction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good causes and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"Good cause required; 3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)). There is a further requirement that the owner give at least three (3) working days’ notice to the County Welfare Board before starting the eviction action for any resident (N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for owner-occupancy and demolition eviction violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months; a landlord who evicts to demolish or retire a building may not use it residentially for five years. Violations bring treble damages, fees and up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use ban; treble damages; up to $10,000 per violation","coverage_conditions":"Applies where a tenant vacated after notice that the landlord wished to personally occupy the unit or to permanently board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Liable to the former tenant for three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs  (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal — civil rights of tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains to a government authority about health or safety violations, joins a tenant organization, or refuses a lease change made in reprisal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All dwelling rentals except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"If a landlord does take reprisal action, the tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure: 90-day notice to quit and post-sale tenant notices","requirement":"Before a foreclosure eviction, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit when the buyer intends to occupy the property; a new owner of a foreclosed building with rental units must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice of sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in properties that have been foreclosed; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirement.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"The packet states federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"A landlord converting a building to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership may not evict eligible senior, disabled or certain veteran tenants; qualifying tenants with limited income are protected from eviction for 40 years, and applications must generally be filed within 60 days.","key_value":"40 years' protection; 60-day filing; income up to 3× county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 years old before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled tenants, or honorably discharged veterans 60% or more disabled from service, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (qualified counties)","requirement":"In qualified counties, the Act extends eviction protections to tenants in buildings converted or being converted to condominium or cooperative ownership who were not eligible for protected tenancy under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Qualified counties only; currently Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibited practice: rent increase above rent control or unconscionable increase","requirement":"A landlord may not, as a prohibited practice against a tenant, raise rent above the applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not rent controlled, impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Coverage is not stated: the introductory paragraph of this prohibited-practice list is omitted from this packet part.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Unclear; introductory text of the prohibited-practice list is omitted from this packet"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"NJ Dep't of Cmty. Affairs, Truth in Renting (prohibited practices list)","quoted_span":"5. An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
